# KLAGON.org security headers (Cloudflare Pages / Netlify `_headers` format).
# Next.js copies public/_headers into the static export. If the host does
# not support this file, set the same headers in the host dashboard.
#
# CSP notes: Next.js static export inlines its flight-data scripts, so
# script-src must allow 'unsafe-inline' (no nonces in static hosting).
# XSS backstop is DOMPurify on all innerHTML sinks (see src/lib/sanitize).
# JSON-LD blocks are exempt (non-executable script types). Styles allow
# 'unsafe-inline' for framework-injected styles + Google Fonts for kocc.
# tiles.openfreemap.org serves the vector basemap (style JSON, glyphs,
# sprites, tiles) — blocking it blanks the /map basemap.
# challenges.cloudflare.com must stay allowed for Cloudflare Turnstile:
#   - script-src: the api.js loader (without it window.turnstile never appears)
#   - frame-src:  the widget's challenge iframe
#   - connect-src: the verify worker that runs server-side siteverify
# Without all three, every Turnstile-gated form (register, contact, mentor,
# sponsor, field, walk, posts, chat) shows "complete the human check" forever.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), microphone=(self)
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.supabase.co https://tiles.openfreemap.org https://images.pexels.com; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co https://klagon-sales-agent.gideonabochie.workers.dev https://klagon-payments.gideonabochie.workers.dev https://tiles.openfreemap.org https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src 'self' https://www.youtube.com https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# RSC flight payloads. `output: "export"` makes Next.js write one
# <route>/index.txt beside every index.html (987 of them). They are the React
# Server Components wire format, served as text/plain, and exist only so
# client-side <Link> prefetching works. A human landing on one sees gibberish,
# so they must never be indexed.
#
# X-Robots-Tag rather than a robots.txt Disallow, deliberately: Disallow only
# prevents crawling, it does not remove a URL that is already indexed or that
# arrives via an external link. noindex is what de-indexes. This has no effect
# on the browser fetch() that prefetching depends on.
#
# Do not delete or block these files. They are not optional cache warming --
# removing them turns every <Link> into a full page load.
/*.txt
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# Fingerprinted build assets.
#
# Measured against production before this rule existed: every asset under
# /_next/static was served `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate` with
# `cf-cache-status: REVALIDATED`. Every file in these three directories carries
# a content hash in its filename, so a changed file is a changed URL. Caching
# them immutably is the standard treatment and costs nothing in correctness:
#
#   - The homepage alone pulls 18 assets totalling ~914 KB. At max-age=14400
#     every returning visitor revalidated all of it against the edge every four
#     hours, which turns a cached page into a round trip per asset.
#   - The hash is what makes it safe. Deploying a change produces a new
#     filename, so a long-lived cache entry is never stale.
#
# `immutable` is doing real work beyond max-age: it stops the browser from
# revalidating on reload even when the entry is still fresh.
#
# Scoped to the three directories that are 100% content-hashed, and asserted to
# stay that way at build time by scripts/verify-cache-headers.mjs. Deliberately
# NOT `/_next/static/*`, which would also cover the build-id directory holding
# `_buildManifest.js` and `_ssgManifest.js`. Those two are NOT hashed, so
# `immutable` on them could pin a visitor to a stale build.
#
# One year, matching Cloudflare's documented example for fingerprinted assets.
/_next/static/chunks/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/_next/static/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/_next/static/media/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two unhashed build files need no rule. They live under the build-id
# directory, which none of the rules above match, so they keep Cloudflare's
# default. That default is correct for them: they are rewritten whenever a route
# is added or removed, and a stale manifest can strand a visitor on a build that
# no longer exists. Verified unnecessary to override by tracing a cold homepage
# load: 34 requests to /_next/static, every one content-hashed, neither manifest
# among them.
#
# HTML is likewise left alone. It is prerendered at build time, so a long-lived
# HTML cache would pin visitors to whatever content existed at deploy, and
# Cloudflare's default (max-age=0, must-revalidate) is already right.
